namespace Eigen {

/** \page TopicUsingAOCL Using AMD® AOCL from %Eigen, or the Enlistment of Outside Assistance in the Business of Arithmetic

Since %Eigen version 3.4 and later, users can benefit from built-in AMD® Optimizing CPU Libraries (AOCL) optimizations with an installed copy of AOCL 5.0 (or later), a circumstance which will be received with satisfaction by every reader who has ever wished that someone else might be found to do the arithmetic.

<a href="https://www.amd.com/en/developer/aocl.html"> AMD AOCL </a> provides highly optimized, multi-threaded mathematical routines for x86-64 processors with a focus on AMD "Zen"-based architectures. AOCL is available on Linux and Windows for x86-64 architectures, and may be had in either of those two provinces.

\note
AMD® AOCL is freely available software, but it is the responsibility of users to download, install, and ensure their product's license allows linking to the AOCL libraries; the law, in these matters, being no respecter of the ignorant. AOCL is distributed under a permissive license that allows commercial use.

Using AMD AOCL through %Eigen is straightforward, being a matter of four steps, which the reader is requested to take in their order:
-# export \c AOCL_ROOT into your environment, that the Library may know in what quarter to apply 
-# define one of the AOCL macros before including any %Eigen headers (see table below), and not an instant after
-# link your program to AOCL libraries (BLIS, FLAME, LibM), those three eminent houses
-# ensure your system supports the target architecture optimizations, a point upon which the linker will not take your word

When doing so, a number of %Eigen's algorithms are silently substituted with calls to AMD AOCL routines, as a clerk, without a word to the public, passes certain of his duties to a more eminent colleague.
These substitutions apply only for \b Dynamic \b or \b large \b enough objects with one of the following standard scalar types: \c float, \c double, \c complex<float>, and \c complex<double>.
Operations on other scalar types or mixing reals and complexes will continue to use the built-in algorithms, the home establishment being always ready to receive them.

The AOCL integration targets three core components, being the three principal gentlemen of the concern:
- **BLIS**: High-performance BLAS implementation optimized for modern cache hierarchies, and a person of the most refined and exacting habits
- **FLAME**: Dense linear algebra algorithms providing LAPACK functionality, upon whom the decompositions devolve  
- **LibM**: Optimized standard math routines with vectorized implementations, who keeps the tables of sines and logarithms

\section TopicUsingAOCL_Macros Configuration Macros, the Several Bell-Pulls

You can choose which parts will be substituted by defining one or multiple of the following macros, which are the bells by which each outside helper is summoned:

<table class="manual">
<tr><td>\c EIGEN_USE_BLAS </td><td>Enables the use of external BLAS level 2 and 3 routines (AOCL-BLIS), calling in the specialist</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>\c EIGEN_USE_LAPACKE </td><td>Enables the use of external LAPACK routines via the LAPACKE C interface (AOCL-FLAME), by way of the C front door</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c EIGEN_USE_LAPACKE_STRICT </td><td>Same as \c EIGEN_USE_LAPACKE but algorithms of lower robustness are disabled, the less trustworthy being turned away. \n This currently concerns only JacobiSVD which would be replaced by \c gesvd.</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>\c EIGEN_USE_AOCL_VML </td><td>Enables the use of AOCL LibM vector math operations for coefficient-wise functions, the tables being consulted in bulk</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c EIGEN_USE_AOCL_ALL </td><td>Defines \c EIGEN_USE_BLAS, \c EIGEN_USE_LAPACKE, and \c EIGEN_USE_AOCL_VML, summoning the whole household with a single pull</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>\c EIGEN_USE_AOCL_MT </td><td>Equivalent to \c EIGEN_USE_AOCL_ALL, but ensures multi-threaded BLIS (\c libblis-mt) is used, the specialist bringing his assistants. \n \b Recommended for most applications.</td></tr>
</table>

\note The AOCL integration automatically enables optimizations when the matrix/vector size exceeds \c EIGEN_AOCL_VML_THRESHOLD (default: 128 elements). For smaller operations, Eigen's built-in vectorization may be faster due to function call overhead, it being a poor economy to send for a specialist to add two and two.

\section TopicUsingAOCL_Performance Performance Considerations, Touching the Speed of the Whole Proceeding

The \c EIGEN_USE_BLAS and \c EIGEN_USE_LAPACKE macros can be combined with AOCL-specific optimizations, which are as follows:

- **Multi-threading**: Use \c EIGEN_USE_AOCL_MT to automatically select the multi-threaded BLIS library, and put many hands to the work
- **Architecture targeting**: AOCL libraries are optimized for AMD Zen architectures (Zen, Zen2, Zen3, Zen4, Zen5), being, as it were, tailored to the measure of that particular family
- **Vector Math Library**: AOCL LibM provides vectorized implementations that can operate on entire arrays simultaneously, a whole regiment passing the gate at once
- **Memory layout**: Eigen's column-major storage directly matches AOCL's expected data layout for zero-copy operation, so that the goods need not be unpacked and repacked at the frontier

\section TopicUsingAOCL_Types Supported Data Types and Sizes, being the Qualifications for Admission

AOCL acceleration is applied to those who answer to the following description:
- **Scalar types**: \c float, \c double, \c complex<float>, \c complex<double>, being the four who are received
- **Matrix/Vector sizes**: Dynamic size or compile-time size ≥ \c EIGEN_AOCL_VML_THRESHOLD, none below that stature being worth the call  
- **Storage order**: Both column-major (default) and row-major layouts, the specialist being of no party in that dispute
- **Memory alignment**: Eigen's data pointers are directly compatible with AOCL function signatures, requiring no letter of introduction

The current AOCL Vector Math Library integration is specialized for \c double precision, with automatic fallback to scalar implementations for \c float, who is thus handled by the household in the ordinary way.

\section TopicUsingAOCL_Functions Vector Math Functions, with Their Corresponding Tradesmen

The following table summarizes coefficient-wise operations accelerated by \c EIGEN_USE_AOCL_VML, with the name of the tradesman who undertakes each:

<table class="manual">
<tr><th>Code example</th><th>AOCL routines</th></tr>
<tr><td>\code
v2 = v1.array().exp();
v2 = v1.array().sin();
v2 = v1.array().cos();
v2 = v1.array().tan();
v2 = v1.array().log();
v2 = v1.array().log10();
v2 = v1.array().log2();
v2 = v1.array().sqrt();
v2 = v1.array().pow(1.5);
v2 = v1.array() + v2.array();
\endcode</td><td>\code
amd_vrda_exp
amd_vrda_sin
amd_vrda_cos
amd_vrda_tan
amd_vrda_log
amd_vrda_log10
amd_vrda_log2
amd_vrda_sqrt
amd_vrda_pow
amd_vrda_add
\endcode</td></tr>
</table>

In the examples, v1 and v2 are dense vectors of type \c VectorXd with size ≥ \c EIGEN_AOCL_VML_THRESHOLD, and of no other description.

\section TopicUsingAOCL_Example Complete Example, Offered for the Reader's Instruction and Amusement

\code
#define EIGEN_USE_AOCL_MT
#include <iostream>
#include <Eigen/Dense>

int main() {
    const int n = 2048;
    
    // Large matrices automatically use AOCL-BLIS for multiplication
    Eigen::MatrixXd A = Eigen::MatrixXd::Random(n, n);
    Eigen::MatrixXd B = Eigen::MatrixXd::Random(n, n);
    Eigen::MatrixXd C = A * B;  // Dispatched to dgemm
    
    // Large vectors automatically use AOCL LibM for math functions
    Eigen::VectorXd v = Eigen::VectorXd::LinSpaced(10000, 0, 10);
    Eigen::VectorXd result = v.array().sin();  // Dispatched to amd_vrda_sin
    
    // LAPACK decompositions use AOCL-FLAME
    Eigen::LLT<Eigen::MatrixXd> llt(A);  // Dispatched to dpotrf
    
    std::cout << "Matrix norm: " << C.norm() << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Vector result norm: " << result.norm() << std::endl;
    
    return 0;
}
\endcode

\section TopicUsingAOCL_Building Building and Linking, the Business of Putting the Parties in Correspondence

To compile with AOCL support, set the \c AOCL_ROOT environment variable and link against the required libraries, the linker being a stern official who will accept no substitute:

\code
export AOCL_ROOT=/path/to/aocl
clang++ -O3 -g -DEIGEN_USE_AOCL_ALL \
        -I./install/include -I${AOCL_ROOT}/include \
        -Wno-parentheses my_app.cpp \
        -L${AOCL_ROOT} -lamdlibm -lflame -lblis \
        -lpthread -lrt -lm -lomp \
        -o eigen_aocl_example
\endcode

For multi-threaded performance, use the multi-threaded BLIS library, the specialist bringing his assistants:
\code
clang++ -O3 -g -DEIGEN_USE_AOCL_MT \
        -I./install/include -I${AOCL_ROOT}/include \
        -Wno-parentheses my_app.cpp \
        -L${AOCL_ROOT} -lamdlibm -lflame -lblis-mt \
        -lpthread -lrt -lm -lomp \
        -o eigen_aocl_example
\endcode

Key compiler and linker flags, which every careful traveller will have by heart:
- \c -DEIGEN_USE_AOCL_ALL: Enable all AOCL accelerations (BLAS, LAPACK, VML), the whole connection at once
- \c -DEIGEN_USE_AOCL_MT: Enable multi-threaded version (uses \c -lblis-mt), with the many hands
- \c -lblis: Single-threaded BLIS library, being the specialist who works alone
- \c -lblis-mt: Multi-threaded BLIS library (recommended for performance), being the specialist with his whole staff
- \c -lflame: FLAME LAPACK implementation, a respectable house for the decompositions  
- \c -lamdlibm: AMD LibM vector math library, keeper of the tables
- \c -lomp: OpenMP runtime for multi-threading support, the clerk who apportions the labor among the hands
- \c -lpthread -lrt: System threading and real-time libraries, being the plain furniture of the system
- \c -Wno-parentheses: Suppress common warnings when using AOCL headers, which are given to a great deal of unnecessary remark

\subsection TopicUsingAOCL_EigenBuild Building Eigen with AOCL Support, in Which the Library Itself Is Prepared for the Visit

To build Eigen with AOCL Support, use the following CMake configuration, which the reader is advised to take down exactly as it is given:

\code
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
         -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang \
         -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
         -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$PWD/install \
         -DINCLUDE_INSTALL_DIR=$PWD/install/include \
      && make install -j$(nproc)
\endcode


To build Eigen with AOCL integration, use the following CMake configuration (the same, it will be observed, as before; for the Library is nothing if not consistent):

\code
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
         -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang \
         -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
         -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$PWD/install \
         -DINCLUDE_INSTALL_DIR=$PWD/install/include \
      && make install -j$(nproc)
\endcode

**CMake Configuration Parameters, Which May Be Consulted at Leisure:**

<table class="manual">
<tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Expected Values</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>\c CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE</td><td>\c Release, \c Debug, \c RelWithDebInfo</td><td>Build configuration (\c Release recommended for benchmarks, where speed is the whole object of the exercise)</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>\c CMAKE_C_COMPILER</td><td>\c clang, \c gcc</td><td>C compiler (clang recommended for AOCL, as the most congenial partner)</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER</td><td>\c clang++, \c g++</td><td>C++ compiler (clang++ recommended for AOCL, for the same reason)</td></tr>
<tr class="alt"><td>\c CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX</td><td>Installation path</td><td>Where to install Eigen headers, being the address at which the Library will receive callers</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c INCLUDE_INSTALL_DIR</td><td>Header path</td><td>Specific path for Eigen headers, being the very room in which they are kept</td></tr>
</table>

**Architecture Selection Guide, or Which Family Does the Reader Belong To?**
- \c znver3: AMD Zen 3 (EPYC 7003, Ryzen 5000 series)
- \c znver4: AMD Zen 4 (EPYC 9004, Ryzen 7000 series)  
- \c znver5: AMD Zen 5 (EPYC 9005, Ryzen 9000 series)
- \c native: Auto-detect current CPU architecture, the compiler being left to inquire for itself
- \c generic: Generic x86-64 without specific optimizations, being the plain suit that will go anywhere

**Custom Compiler Flags Explanation, with Notes Upon Each:**
- \c -O3: Maximum optimization level, the clerk set to work with the utmost diligence
- \c -mavx512f: Enable AVX-512 instruction set (if supported), the machine having, in that case, a very wide gate
- \c -fveclib=AMDLIBM: Use AMD LibM for vectorized math functions, and let the tradesman do the sines

\subsection TopicUsingAOCL_Benchmark Building the AOCL Benchmark, a Trial of Strength

After configuring Eigen, build the AOCL benchmark executable, which will be found a candid and impartial witness:

\code
cmake --build . --target benchmark_aocl -j$(nproc)
\endcode

This creates the \c benchmark_aocl executable that demonstrates AOCL acceleration with various matrix sizes and operations, so that the reader need not take anybody's word for it.

**Running the Benchmark, and Awaiting the Verdict:**
\code
./benchmark_aocl
\endcode

The benchmark will automatically compare, with perfect impartiality:
- Eigen's native performance vs AOCL-accelerated operations, the home team against the visitors
- Matrix multiplication performance (BLIS vs Eigen), the heavy-weight contest
- Vector math functions performance (LibM vs Eigen), a trial of the tables
- Memory bandwidth utilization and cache efficiency, being the state of the roads and of the larder

\section TopicUsingAOCL_CMake CMake Integration, for Those Who Prefer to Be Introduced by a Third Party

When using CMake, you can use a FindAOCL module, which performs the introductions:

\code
find_package(AOCL REQUIRED)
target_compile_definitions(my_target PRIVATE EIGEN_USE_AOCL_MT)
target_link_libraries(my_target PRIVATE AOCL::BLIS_MT AOCL::FLAME AOCL::LIBM)
\endcode

\section TopicUsingAOCL_Troubleshooting Troubleshooting, or What to Do When the Business Does Not Prosper

Common issues and solutions, with which a long experience of misfortune has made the profession familiar:

- **Link errors**: Ensure \c AOCL_ROOT is set and libraries are in \c LD_LIBRARY_PATH, the linker being unable to find a creditor who has not left his address
- **Performance not improved**: Verify you're using matrices/vectors larger than the threshold, the specialist declining to attend upon trifles
- **Thread contention**: Set \c OMP_NUM_THREADS to match your CPU core count, there being no use in engaging more hands than there are benches
- **Architecture mismatch**: Use appropriate \c -march flag for your AMD processor, that the coat may be cut to the wearer

\section TopicUsingAOCL_Links Links, for Further Inquiry

- AMD AOCL can be downloaded for free <a href="https://www.amd.com/en/developer/aocl.html">here</a>, without any charge whatever
- AOCL User Guide and documentation available on the AMD Developer Portal, for the studious
- AOCL is also available through package managers and containerized environments, by whichever road the reader finds most convenient

*/

}
